{
 "id": 33,
 "number": 981,
 "title": "Time Based Key-Value Store",
 "slug": "time-based-key-value-store",
 "category": "binary-search",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/time-based-key-value-store/",
 "pattern": "Hash map of time-sorted lists + binary search",
 "statement": "Design a store with `set(key, value, timestamp)` and `get(key, timestamp)`. `get` returns the value that was set for `key` at the **latest time ≤ timestamp**, or an empty string if nothing had been set by then. Timestamps passed to `set` always increase.",
 "example": {
  "input": "TimeMap()\nset('price', 'low', 1)\nset('price', 'mid', 4)\nset('price', 'high', 8)\nget('price', 5)  →  \"mid\"\nget('price', 8)  →  \"high\"\nget('price', 0)  →  \"\"",
  "output": "",
  "why": "At time 5 the latest value is the one set at time 4. At time 0 nothing had been set yet."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "TimeMap()\nset('foo', 'bar', 1)\nget('foo', 1)  →  \"bar\"\nget('foo', 3)  →  \"bar\"\nset('foo', 'bar2', 4)\nget('foo', 4)  →  \"bar2\"\nget('foo', 5)  →  \"bar2\"\nget('zzz', 5)  →  \"\"",
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Keep, for every key, a list of (timestamp, value) pairs. Because timestamps only increase, each list is **already sorted** by time.",
  "`get` needs the last pair whose timestamp is ≤ the one asked for — a search in a sorted list, so use binary search.",
  "When the search ends without an exact hit, `right` has stopped on the last entry that is not too new. If `right` fell off the left edge, there is no such entry."
 ],
 "code": "class TimeMap:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.data = defaultdict(list)\n\n    def set(self, key: str, value: str, timestamp: int) -> None:\n        self.data[key].append((timestamp, value))\n\n    def get(self, key: str, timestamp: int) -> str:\n        values = self.data[key]\n        left, right = 0, len(values) - 1\n\n        while left <= right:\n            mid = left + (right - left) // 2\n            if values[mid][0] == timestamp:\n                return values[mid][1]\n            elif values[mid][0] < timestamp:\n                left = mid + 1\n            else:\n                right = mid - 1\n\n        if right >= 0:\n            return values[right][1]\n        return \"\"",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(log n) per get",
  "timeWhy": "`set` appends in O(1); `get` binary-searches the n entries of one key.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "Every value ever set is stored."
 },
